    "category": "Hematology: G6PD Episode After Anti-malarial",
    "questionText": "A 28-year-old man of Mediterranean descent travels to sub-Saharan Africa and is given PRIMAQUINE for radical cure of P. vivax malaria (treating presumed hypnozoites). Twenty-four hours after his first dose, he develops dark urine, jaundice, abdominal pain, and weakness. Vitals: T 38.0°C, BP 100/60, HR 110, RR 22. Exam: pale, scleral icterus, mild abdominal tenderness, no hepatosplenomegaly. Labs: Hgb 7.5 (baseline 14), MCV 95, retic 12%, total bilirubin 5.0 (indirect 4.5), LDH 1,400, haptoglobin <10, peripheral smear shows BITE CELLS and HEMIGHOSTS, Heinz bodies on supravital staining. Which is the most appropriate management?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Continue primaquine to complete the antimalarial course; supportive care", "explanation": "Incorrect. Primaquine is the TRIGGER of acute hemolysis in this G6PD-deficient patient and must be DISCONTINUED. The MEDITERRANEAN variant of G6PD is much more severe than the African (A-) variant — hemolysis can be life-threatening. This would be the correct answer if the patient had no G6PD issues and tolerated the drug." },
      { "text": "Discontinue primaquine; hydrate; confirm G6PD ≥3 months later", "explanation": "Correct. Supportive care with hydration to prevent pigment nephropathy, monitor for renal function and potassium, transfuse if symptomatic anemia worsens; confirm G6PD status with quantitative assay AFTER acute hemolysis has subsided (≥3 months). This is G6PD DEFICIENCY HEMOLYTIC CRISIS triggered by PRIMAQUINE (a well-known oxidative drug). G6PD is a key enzyme in the pentose phosphate pathway generating NADPH — required to maintain reduced glutathione that detoxifies oxidative stress. Without G6PD, oxidant exposure causes hemoglobin oxidation (Heinz bodies = oxidized denatured hemoglobin), splenic macrophages 'bite out' the precipitates (bite cells), and severe hemolysis ensues. The Mediterranean variant has very low residual enzyme activity (<10% of normal) and causes severe, prolonged hemolysis. Management: (1) DISCONTINUE the offending drug; (2) Supportive care: IV hydration to prevent pigment nephropathy (free hemoglobin can damage renal tubules); (3) Monitor renal function and potassium (hemolysis releases K+); (4) Transfuse if Hgb continues to fall and patient is symptomatic; (5) G6PD ASSAY: do NOT do during the acute crisis — the most deficient RBCs have already been destroyed, leaving only younger reticulocytes that are relatively G6PD-replete, producing a FALSELY NORMAL or NEAR-NORMAL G6PD level. Wait at least 3 months (until reticulocytes have aged into mature RBCs) for accurate measurement. (6) PROVIDE PATIENT EDUCATION on drugs/foods to avoid: SULFA drugs (TMP-SMX, sulfasalazine, dapsone), antimalarials (primaquine, tafenoquine — should screen G6PD BEFORE these), nitrofurantoin, methylene blue, fava beans, and certain henna products. Always screen G6PD BEFORE prescribing primaquine or tafenoquine." },
      { "text": "Initiate eculizumab to prevent further complement-mediated hemolysis", "explanation": "Incorrect. G6PD hemolysis is OXIDATIVE, not complement-mediated. Eculizumab targets complement (PNH, atypical HUS, cold agglutinin disease), not oxidative hemolysis. This would be the correct answer in PNH with thrombosis." },
      { "text": "Start prednisone 1 mg/kg for presumed autoimmune hemolytic anemia", "explanation": "Incorrect. G6PD hemolysis is NOT autoimmune — Coombs test would be NEGATIVE (intrinsic enzyme defect, not antibody-mediated). The bite cells, Heinz bodies, and drug trigger point clearly to G6PD. Steroids would not help. This would be the correct answer in warm AIHA with positive direct Coombs." },
      { "text": "Plasmapheresis to remove oxidized hemoglobin and offending drug", "explanation": "Incorrect. Plasmapheresis is not standard for G6PD hemolysis — supportive care after stopping the drug is the management. The drug is largely metabolized within hours and oxidized hemoglobin clearance is by macrophages. This would be the correct answer in TTP." }
    ],
