    "category": "Hematology: HbSC vs HbSS",
    "questionText": "A 24-year-old African-American woman presents to clinic with vision loss in her right eye and proliferative retinopathy on funduscopic exam. PMH: occasional bone pain episodes, no hospitalization for crises, no transfusions. She has never had splenic sequestration or acute chest syndrome. Vitals: BP 122/74, HR 80. Exam: spleen palpable 4 cm below costal margin (preserved into adulthood — unusual for HbSS), proliferative retinopathy in right eye, normal cardiovascular exam. Labs: Hgb 11.5 (HIGHER than typical HbSS), MCV 72, retic 5%, smear shows TARGET CELLS and a few sickled cells. Hb electrophoresis: HbS 50%, HbC 50%, HbA absent. Which best describes her condition?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Sickle cell disease (HbSS) with unusually mild course", "explanation": "Incorrect. HbSS patients have NO HbC; electrophoresis would show HbS predominant (~90%), HbF variable (5-15%), and NO HbA. Also, HbSS patients typically have autosplenectomized by adulthood and have lower baseline Hgb (~6-9). This patient's electrophoresis (50% HbS + 50% HbC) and preserved spleen point clearly to HbSC. This would be the correct answer with HbS predominant and absent HbC." },
      { "text": "HbSC disease (compound heterozygous sickle and HbC)", "explanation": "Correct. HbSC is milder than HbSS overall but has higher rates of proliferative retinopathy, splenic complications, and avascular necrosis. HbSC disease is the compound heterozygous combination of one HbS allele and one HbC allele. Clinical features distinguishing it from HbSS: (1) MILDER OVERALL — less severe anemia (Hgb often 9-12, vs HbSS ~6-9), fewer pain crises, fewer hospitalizations; (2) PRESERVED SPLEEN longer (often into adulthood — unlike HbSS where autosplenectomy occurs in childhood) → at risk for splenic sequestration even into adolescence/adulthood; (3) PROLIFERATIVE SICKLE RETINOPATHY is significantly more common in HbSC than HbSS (related to higher Hb levels and increased blood viscosity in retinal vessels); (4) AVASCULAR NECROSIS of the femoral head is more common; (5) Hematuria from medullary infarction; (6) Pregnancy can be more complicated. Hb electrophoresis is diagnostic: 50% HbS + 50% HbC + absent HbA. Management: similar principles to HbSS — annual eye exams (especially looking for proliferative retinopathy → may need laser photocoagulation), hydroxyurea consideration, pain crisis management, vaccinations against encapsulated organisms (functional asplenia still develops eventually). HbC alone (homozygous) produces only mild hemolytic anemia." },
      { "text": "Sickle cell trait (HbAS)", "explanation": "Incorrect. Sickle cell trait shows HbA predominant (~60%) + HbS (~40%), with NO HbC. Sickle trait is generally asymptomatic. This would be the correct answer with HbA-predominant electrophoresis and minimal symptoms." },
      { "text": "Beta-thalassemia major", "explanation": "Incorrect. Beta-thal major shows ABSENT HbA + markedly elevated HbF + elevated HbA2, with severe transfusion-dependent anemia from childhood. No HbS or HbC. This would be the correct answer with absent HbA, elevated HbF and HbA2, and chronic transfusion dependence." },
      { "text": "HbCC disease (homozygous HbC)", "explanation": "Incorrect. HbCC homozygous would show 100% HbC + no HbS + no HbA on electrophoresis. The patient has 50% HbS, ruling out HbCC. HbC alone causes only mild hemolytic anemia with target cells; the SC compound has its distinct features. This would be the correct answer with 100% HbC and mild hemolytic anemia." }
    ],
